- Contributing to experimentation
    - This guide walks a new contributor through cloning, setting up a venv, registering a Jupyter kernel, regenerating chapter artifacts, and rendering the loglog narratives. Read the README.md for the conceptual layout first.
- Local setup
    - Python 3.11 or 3.12. Other versions probably work but are not tested in CI.
    - Clone and bootstrap a project-local virtualenv:
        - ```
        - git clone <repo>
        - cd experimentation
        - python -m venv .venv
        - source .venv/bin/activate
        - pip install --upgrade pip
        - pip install -e ".[dev]"
        - ```
    - The package installs in editable mode, so your changes to expkit/ are reflected immediately without re-installing.
- Jupyter kernel for the notebooks
    - Each chapter has a notebook.ipynb that imports from expkit. The notebooks specify a kernel named "expkit" so the venv-installed package is found. Register it once:
        - ```
        - .venv/bin/python -m ipykernel install --user --name expkit --display-name "Python (expkit)"
        - ```
    - You can re-register safely; subsequent installs replace the existing kernel spec.
    - To execute a notebook end-to-end without opening Jupyter:
        - ```
        - python -c "import nbformat; from nbclient import NotebookClient; nb = nbformat.read('chapters/01-the-coin/notebook.ipynb', as_version=4); NotebookClient(nb, kernel_name='expkit', timeout=600).execute(); nbformat.write(nb, 'chapters/01-the-coin/notebook.ipynb')"
        - ```
    - Or, for all notebooks at once: ``make notebooks``.
- Loglog rendering
    - The narrative source for every chapter and the README is a .log file in loglog format. The rendered .md is committed alongside it so GitHub displays the story directly.
    - To render every .log file in the repo: ``make render`` (which calls ``python scripts/render_chapters.py``).
    - To render only one chapter: ``python scripts/render_chapters.py --chapter 01-the-coin``.
    - The renderer expects the loglog CLI from the sibling repo at /home/k1/public/loglog/loglog_cli.py. If you have your own loglog installation on PATH, the script auto-detects it.
- Regenerating chapter artifacts
    - Every chapter has a generate.py that writes datasets and PyMC traces to chapters/<NN-name>/data/, plots to chapters/<NN-name>/images/, and updates the cross-cutting data/manifest.yaml.
    - To regenerate everything: ``make regen``.
    - To regenerate one chapter: ``python scripts/regenerate_all.py --chapter 01-the-coin``.
    - The manifest records every artifact with its seed and sha256. CI can be used to assert these checksums match by re-running regenerate_all and diffing the manifest.
- Tests
    - Fast tests run in seconds: ``make test``.
    - Including PyMC sampling: ``make test-all``. Adds ~10 seconds for the conjugate-vs-MCMC convergence test.
    - When adding a new expkit module, add a tests/test_<module>.py with at least:
        - happy-path numeric checks (cross-check against scipy or statsmodels where possible)
        - shape and dtype invariants
        - determinism with a fixed seed
        - one or two boundary cases
- Privacy policy
    - This repo has a strict privacy policy: no traces of any specific AI tooling in committed files or commit messages. The check is ``make privacy`` (which calls ``scripts/check_privacy.sh``) and the same check runs in CI.
    - The .gitignore is the only allowed exception, because it must literally name the paths it ignores.
    - The full policy lives in the local-only instructions file (gitignored). The short form: do not introduce strings that name AI tooling vendors or models, and do not add AI-coauthor trailer lines to commit messages.
    - One-time setup: run ``bash scripts/install_pre_commit.sh`` once after cloning. This wires ``.git/hooks/pre-commit`` to the privacy guard so violations are caught locally before they reach CI. The hook lives only in your local clone (``.git`` is not tracked); each contributor installs it themselves.
- Project structure (quick map)
    - chapters/<NN-name>/ -- one folder per chapter; chapter.log + chapter.md + notebook.ipynb + generate.py + data/ + images/
    - expkit/ -- the Python package: sim/, inference/, power/, metrics/, segments/, novelty/, attribution/, plot/, io/
    - tests/ -- pytest unit tests for expkit modules
    - scripts/ -- regenerate_all.py, render_chapters.py, check_privacy.sh
    - data/manifest.yaml -- cross-cutting reproducibility manifest
    - .github/workflows/ -- CI configuration
    - Makefile -- convenience targets for the common workflows
- A typical contribution flow
    - Open an issue or PR describing the change.
    - Edit the relevant chapter.log, then ``make render`` to update the corresponding chapter.md.
    - If you changed library code: add or update the test file, ``make test``.
    - If you changed a generator: ``make regen`` so the manifest and figures stay consistent.
    - If you changed a notebook: ``make notebooks`` (or just the one notebook) so its outputs are up to date.
    - ``make privacy`` before committing.
    - Commit with a plain message in the user's voice. No AI tool trailers.
- Questions and feedback
    - Please open an issue for anything that's confusing, broken, or missing in the narrative or the library. The chapters are still polishing toward "Production"; the more concrete the question, the better the polish.
